{"entries":{"chapter-67-study-palliative-care-and-special-management-issues|Chapter 67 Study: Palliative Care and Special Management Issues":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"definition-amp-scope|Definition & scope":["u3"],"copy|Palliative care addresses serious-illness symptoms, stress and quality of life for patients and families; it can accompany curative treatment at any stage. Primary palliative care includes symptom care, coordination, communication and caregiver support. (p. 1045)":["u4"],"copy|US Medicare hospice requires a prognosis of six months or less if the illness runs its usual course. Initial certification comes from the hospice physician and, if the patient has a designated attending physician, that physician. Coverage guidelines support clinical judgment (p1046, CMS Medicare Benefit Policy Manual, chapter 9).":["u5"],"copy|Trials support quality-of-life and family benefits; noncancer evidence includes less emergency use, hospitalization and symptom burden. Dementia trials have been least promising. (p. 1047)":["u6"],"assessment-amp-planning|Assessment & planning":["u7"],"copy|Prognostication involves estimating, communicating and interpreting prognosis; compare time to benefit with life expectancy. (p. 1050)":["u8"],"copy|Clinicians tend to overestimate survival by 3-5-fold. Combine clinical information, cognition, function and prognostic indices; acknowledge uncertainty. (p. 1051)":["u9"],"copy|ACP is an ongoing conversation. In the US framework, a proxy designates a decision maker, a living will states future wishes, and POLST records current treatment orders. (p. 1052)":["u10"],"management-amp-drugs|Management & drugs":["u11"],"copy|Advanced dementia: offer assisted oral feeding; feeding tubes have not improved survival, quality of life, aspiration prevention or pressure-ulcer healing. (p. 1048)":["u12"],"copy|Pain may drive behavioral symptoms: acetaminophen was the first step for most patients in a stepwise trial; opioids may be tried for nonresponders. Antipsychotics are not first-line behavioral treatment. (p. 1048)":["u13"],"copy|Aspiration-pneumonia antibiotics in advanced dementia were associated with longer survival, not better comfort; intravenous treatment/hospitalization caused greatest discomfort. Align infection treatment with goals. (p. 1048)":["u14"],"copy|Frailty care: consider reversibility, treatment burden and patient goals; a therapeutic trial can clarify potential, with renewed goals discussion if function does not improve. (p. 1049)":["u15"],"exam-traps|Exam traps":["u16"],"copy|Palliative care does not require stopping curative therapy. (p. 1045)":["u17"],"copy|Frailty's exclusion as a principal US hospice billing diagnosis does not itself exclude hospice eligibility. (p. 1049)":["u18"],"copy|ACP is more than a completed form; preferences may change. (p. 1052)":["u19"],"recall|Recall":["u20"],"past-paper-questions-on-this-chapter|Past-paper questions on this chapter":["u21"],"copy|2023-Jun Q23 — mcq-795f2687afa4d5918ddb65de":["u22"],"copy|2026-Jun Q62 — mcq-4ec2512c26a7775166980a4b":["u23"],"copy|2025-Jun Q38 — mcq-caa086c8705cdeb3606c5536":["u24"],"copy|2025-Jun Q63 — mcq-8b2cfa55422f88f802142d29":["u25"],"copy|CHAPTER 67s / HAZZARD":["u26"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 67; the book is the reference.":["u27"],"copy|Full chapter available: Read Chapter 67.":["u28"],"copy|Fast build from the book; not lane-reviewed.":["u29"],"copy|2.":["u30"],"copy|What distinguishes primary from specialist palliative care?":["u31"],"copy|5.":["u32"],"copy|6.":["u34"],"copy|What should precede medication for dementia-related behavioral symptoms?":["u35"],"copy|7.":["u36"],"copy|What pain-treatment approach reduced behavioral symptoms in a nursing-home trial?":["u37"],"copy|9.":["u38"],"copy|How did aspiration-pneumonia antibiotics affect survival and comfort in advanced dementia?":["u39"],"copy|11.":["u40"],"copy|What domains structure care for multimorbidity?":["u41"],"copy|14.":["u42"],"copy|How can a clinician express prognostic uncertainty?":["u43"],"copy|15.":["u44"],"copy|In the chapter's US framework, how do proxy, living will and POLST differ?":["u45"],"copy|Answer":["u46","u48","u50","u52","u54","u56","u58","u60"],"copy|All clinicians can provide core symptom management, coordination, goals discussions and caregiver support; specialists contribute an interprofessional team with palliative expertise. (p. 1045)":["u47"],"copy|Less emergency department use, hospitalization and symptom burden; dementia trials had been least promising. (p. 1047)":["u49"],"copy|Identify the behavior and severity, identify and remove physiologic/environmental triggers when possible, and attempt nonpharmacologic interventions. (p. 1047)":["u51"],"copy|An empiric stepwise protocol; most patients received acetaminophen at the first step. Opioids may be tried for nonresponders, and PAINAD may help assessment. (p. 1048)":["u53"],"copy|They were associated with improved survival, not improved comfort; intravenous treatment and hospitalization caused greatest discomfort, with similar survival benefit across administration routes. (p. 1048)":["u55"],"copy|Patient preferences, interpretation of evidence, prognosis, clinical feasibility, and optimization of therapies/care plans. (p. 1049)":["u57"],"copy|Give a time range or best-case, worst-case and most common scenarios; support emotional reactions with empathy and therapeutic silence. (p. 1052)":["u59"],"copy|A health care proxy names a future decision maker; a living will records future treatment wishes; POLST is a standing medical order for current care that accompanies the patient across settings. (p. 1052)":["u61"],"copy|Chapter 67 covers symptom relief, goals of care and advance planning on printed pages 1045–1054.":["u29"],"copy|ACP is an ongoing conversation. In the US framework, a health care proxy document designates a decision maker, a living will states future wishes, and POLST records current treatment orders. (p. 1052)":["u10"],"copy|Aspiration-pneumonia antibiotics in advanced dementia were associated with longer survival, not better comfort; intravenous treatment or hospitalization was associated with the greatest discomfort. Align infection treatment with goals. (p. 1048)":["u14"],"copy|They were associated with improved survival, not improved comfort; intravenous treatment or hospitalization was associated with the greatest discomfort, with similar survival benefit across administration routes. (p. 1048)":["u55"],"copy|Hazzard describes an expected prognosis less than six months if the illness runs its usual course, certified by the attending physician and hospice medical director (p1046). The same page describes CMS coverage guidance for a prognosis of six months or less.Current practice (not the book): CMS uses six months or less; for the initial period, the hospice physician and the designated attending physician, if any, certify — CMS, chapter 9, §20.1.":["u5"],"copy|A resident with advanced dementia and aspiration pneumonia may live longer after antibiotics, without improved comfort.":["u103"],"copy|כאשר לא ניתן להאכיל ולהשקות דרך הפה במידה הראויה חולה עם קיהיון הנוטה למות, המותב מנחה לספק מזון ונוזלים בדרך המתאימה למצב, לרבות השלמת נוזלים בעירוי, הזנה בזונדה או ב־PEG (עמ׳ 6, סעיף ח).":["u149"],"copy|Hazzard describes US Medicare hospice eligibility as an expected prognosis less than 6 months on the usual disease course, certified by the attending physician and hospice medical director (p1046).":["u211"],"copy|Services can include medications for the terminal illness, equipment, respite and bereavement support (pp1045–1047).":["u167"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): CMS uses 6 months or less.":["u212"],"copy|Initial certification requires the hospice medical director or hospice interdisciplinary-group physician and the patient’s attending physician if any — Medicare Benefit Policy Manual, ch 9, §20.1.":["u166"],"copy|The chapter reports 17% survival to discharge and 7% discharge home among older inpatients undergoing CPR (p1053).":["u206"],"copy|CHAPTER 67 / HAZZARD":["u26"],"copy|What did the noncancer palliative-care meta-analysis associate with palliative care?":["u33"]},"maxId":212,"sequenceOffsets":[[213,-151],[254,-150],[299,-149],[314,-143],[349,-142],[353,0]]}
